  "scenario": "A host wants an affordable chocolate tasting next month but first wants to understand the route from cocoa bean to finished chocolate, what roasting and conching actually do, what percentage labels can and cannot reveal, and whether dismissing white chocolate as fake is defensible. The frozen shopping packet contains five exact listings: a 22.98-dollar Green & Black's variety pack whose title and detail describe eight 3.17-ounce bars spanning 85-percent dark, 70-percent dark, milk, milk-with-almonds and white, while Product Dimensions reports only 3.17 ounces and the selected flavor field says 70-percent cacao; a 5.39-dollar single Lindt white bar with no mass shown; a 9.58-dollar Lindt Classic milk listing titled 4.4 ounces and two pieces while Product Dimensions reports 4.4 ounces; one 7.99-dollar Ritter Sport 61-percent 100-gram bar rated 55 percent over four reviews; and an 8.99-dollar Chocolove ruby listing titled two bars and selected as a 3.1-ounce two-pack while Product Dimensions reports 3.1 ounces, rated 78 percent over twelve reviews. Background pages describe the bean-to-liquor pathway, cocoa butter, conching, and the composition and disputed category boundaries of white and ruby chocolate. Three community pages contain only one planned local blind caramel comparison, place- and preference-specific hot-cocoa recommendations, and a lay discussion of smell, texture, color and temperature in flavor. No captured source supplies current delivered labels, complete ingredients and allergens for all offers, a matched bar tasting, a verified common percentage definition across every product, or a universal quality ranking.",
